  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:  \n   The goal is to minimize the maximum cross-correlation integral \\( C_5 \\), which captures the worst-case overlap between a step function \\( h(x) \\) and its shifted versions \\( 1 - h(x + k) \\). Given the discretization over \\([0, 2]\\) with \\( n_{\\text{points}} \\) samples, a continuous relaxation of the step function allows optimization in a lower-dimensional space. We represent \\( h \\) as a vector of continuous values in \\([0, 1]\\) (instead of binary) to enable gradient-based optimization using L-BFGS-B, which efficiently handles box constraints. The optimization minimizes \\( C_5 \\) by penalizing overlaps, starting from the parent solution. This approach reduces sidetracking and improves convergence towards a globally optimal or near-optimal solution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:  \n   - **Representation**: Use a 1D array \\( h \\) with \\( n_{\\text{points}} \\) elements, where \\( h[i] \\) corresponds to the value at \\( x_i = i \\cdot dx \\) with \\( dx = 2 / n_{\\text{points}} \\).  \n   - **Constraints**:\n     - \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\).\n     - \\( \\sum h[i] = n_{\\text{points}} / 2 \\).  \n   - **Objective**: Compute \\( C_5 = \\max_k (\\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\"full\")) \\cdot dx \\), where \\( k \\) includes all lag shifts from \\(-(n_{\\text{points}}-1)\\) to \\((n_{\\text{points}}-1)\\).  \n   - **Decomposition**: The objective is nonzero only for lags \\( s \\) in \\(\\{-n+1, \\ldots, n-1\\}\\) (centered at the zero-lag). Only the lag that maximizes the cross-correlation is used for gradient computation, reducing computational cost.  \n   - **Penalty Enforcement**: Uses the L-BFGS-B optimizer's built-in bounds and constraints, avoiding manual projection.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure:  \n   - **Bounds and Constraints**: Define bounds for each \\( h[i] \\) in \\([0, 1]\\). An equality constraint \\( \\sum h[i] = n_{\\text{points}} / 2 \\) ensures the integral is 1.  \n   - **Objective Function**: Compute \\(\\max_k \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\"full\")) \\cdot dx\\).  \n   - **Gradient Function**: For each \\( h[i] \\), compute the gradient using the dominant lag \\( s \\) (returned by np.argmax). The gradient fix at \\( h \\) involves:  \n     \\[\n     \\nabla_h C_5[i] = \\begin{cases}\n     (1 - h[i+s]) & \\text{if } i + s \\text{ is within bounds} \\\\\n     -h[i-s] & \\text{if } i - s \\text{ is within bounds} \\\\\n     0 & \\text{otherwise}\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]  \n   - **Optimization**: Use `scipy.optimize.minimize` with L-BFGS-B, initializing with the parent solution.  \n   - **Fallback**: Upon failure, transition to a sparse representation (e.g., distribute ones periodically) and re-attempt the optimization.  \n\n4. Search choices:  \n   - **Initializations**: Start with the parent solution. If unavailable (other resolutions), generate a periodic pattern.  \n   - **Optimization Stops**: After 100 function evaluations, switch to a penalty-based gradient descent for sparse patterns.  \n   - **Descent Strategy**: L-BFGS-B defaults (bounds, constraints, tolerance).  \n   - **Success Trigger**: Improvement triggers early termination.  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:  \n   - **Runtime**: \\( O(n_{\\text{points}}^2) \\) per objective/gradient evaluation. With \\( n_{\\text{points}} = 70 \\), the evaluation is efficient.  \n   - **Timeout Handling**: Before timeout, revert to the sparse initialization.  \n   - **Feasibility**: The optimizer inherently enforces box constraints and the sum constraint via its 'bounds' and 'constraints' parameters.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract:  \n   - **Check Sum**: Ensure \\( \\sum h[i] = n_{\\text{points}} / 2 \\) exactly.  \n   - **Objective Re-evaluation**: Compute \\(\\max_k\\) separately to avoid floating-point errors.  \n   - **Output**: \\( h_{\\text{values}} \\), \\( C_5 = \\max_k (\\text{corr}) \\cdot dx \\), \\( n_{\\text{points}} \\).  \n   Test on a 2-point grid to justify the gradient formula.",
